<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hazarajat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hazarajat is the mountainous central region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> people, centred on Bamiyan and the highlands of the upper Helmand. Largely self-governing until the 1890s, it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> in brutal campaigns between 1891 and 1893, in which many Hazaras were killed, enslaved or driven from their land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hazarajat.jpg" alt="Hazarajat" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niazof · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hazaristan_(orthographic_projection).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hazarajat, also called Hazaristan, is a mostly mountainous region of the central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, among the Kuh-e Baba mountains at the western end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. Its boundaries have always been inexact and shifting, but it is roughly marked by the Bamiyan Basin to the north, the headwaters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmand-river">Helmand River</a> to the south, Firuzkuh to the west and the Unai Pass to the east. The highest peaks reach between 4,600 and 5,200 m. The rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, Arghandab, Helmand, Hari, Murghab, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a> rise there.</p><p>The north-east is the site of ancient Bamiyan, a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and a key <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserai</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. In the north-west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a> was long known for its mountain fortresses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was ruled successively by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Mauryas</a>, Kushans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> before the Saffarids Islamized it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, Ghaznavids and Ghurids followed, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Genghis Khan and his Mongol army invaded in the 13th century, after which the Qarlughids created a local dynasty that gave a few decades of self-rule. Later the region belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>.</p><p>Until the late 19th century the Hazarajat remained somewhat independent, and only the authority of local chieftains was obeyed. Nomadic Kuchi herders camped there in summer and overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> farmland and pastures. The authority of the Afghan state was extended into the region under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a>, whose government singled out the Hazaras as particularly troublesome, with disastrous consequences for them.</p><p>As the Afghan state weakened in 1979, uprisings freed the Hazarajat from state rule. During the war that followed, local mirs and a new group of young radical commanders ruled it. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> groups united in 1987 and 1989 to form the Hizb-e-Wahdat. Under the Taliban, a revolt broke out in 1997 at Mazar-e Sharif when Hazaras refused to be disarmed, and in 1998 six thousand Hazaras were killed in the north. In March 2001 the two giant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhas-of-bamiyan">Buddhas of Bamiyan</a> were destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hazarajat is the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> people, who form the majority of its population, and denotes an ethnic and religious zone. It consists mainly of the provinces of Bamyan and Daikundi, with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, Zabul, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghor</a>, Maidan Wardak, Sar-e-Pol, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>, Samangan, Uruzgan and Parwan, and it is not an official region. Its people speak Hazaragi, an eastern dialect of Persian with different accents in different districts, among them Behsudi, Jaghori and Daikundi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hazarajat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Second Anglo-Afghan War of 1878 to 1880, Abdur Rahman Khan, the 'Iron Emir', centralised power, crushed revolts and annexed the <b>Hazarajat</b>, while Britain controlled foreign policy.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-afghanistan">Emirate of Afghanistan</a></li></ul></section>
